Final Project: Build the Web
Fundamentals of Web Development
The Goal
The purpose of this project is simple: take the web technologies you learn in this course and use them to design and build something real. Throughout the term you'll learn HTML, CSS, JavaScript, React, server-side scripting, databases, and the practices around usability, accessibility, and security. This project is where you put those pieces together and prove to yourself that you can apply them - not just recognize them on an exam.
You choose what to build. It can be a personal portfolio, a small web app, an interactive tool, a data dashboard, a game, a site for a club or cause you care about - anything that gives you room to show off the technologies from this class. The topic is yours; what matters is that the project genuinely uses the skills we cover.
What You'll Build
Your project must be a working, browser-based web project that meaningfully uses the core technologies of the course. At minimum it should include:
● Structure - A multi-section site or app built with clean, semantic HTML.
● Style - A deliberate, consistent visual design using CSS, including a modern layout technique (flexbox or grid).
● Interactivity - At least one real interactive feature built with JavaScript that does something useful.
● Depth - At least one topic from the second half of the course - React components, a server-side or Node feature, working with data or an API, or state management.
● Craft - Attention to usability, accessibility, and at least one security/good-practice concept from the course.
This is a team project. You'll work in groups of 2-3 - individual projects are not permitted, since collaboration is part of what this project is meant to teach. Every member must contribute meaningfully and be able to explain the whole project, not just their own part.
