In the last week you'll give a short in-class presentation (about 5-7 minutes). Walk us through what you built, why you built it, and - most importantly - which technologies from the course you used and how. A quick live demo is expected. This is your chance to show what you learned to apply.
Team Peer Feedback
Since this is a team project, every member will complete a confidential peer feedback form after the project is turned in. You'll rate each of your teammates - and yourself - on their contribution to the project. The purpose is fairness: a project grade should reflect the work each person actually put in, and this gives everyone a voice in how that's assessed.
You will rate each team member on:
● Effort - How much effort the member put into the project relative to the team.
● Participation - How actively the member participated in meetings, decisions, and communication.
● Contribution - The quality and reliability of the work the member contributed.
● Collaboration - Whether the member was dependable, met deadlines, and was a good teammate.
Each category is scored on a simple scale (for example, 1 = did not contribute, 5 = essential contributor), with space for a short written comment. The form is submitted individually and privately through Canvas - your teammates will not see your ratings.
How it affects grades: the team earns a shared project score from the rubric, but individual grades may be adjusted up or down based on the peer feedback. A member whose peers consistently report they contributed little can receive a reduced share of the project grade; a member who carried more than their share may be recognized. Completing the peer feedback form is itself required - failing to submit it affects your own project grade.
Because every project is a team project, this feedback applies to everyone. You must complete the form for each of your teammates and for yourself; failing to submit it affects your own project grade.
